Connect to the database as SYS user and investigate the package body by running the following command:

SQL> desc DBMS_SPACE


Since your Oracle AI database might have a BIGFILE tablespace type by default, that can be verified by running the following command from SYS user:

SQL> select property_value
from database_properties
where property_name = 'DEFAULT_TBS_TYPE';

Connect as SYS user and run the following command to create a tablespace:

SQL> create smallfile tablespace tbs_shrink_test
datafile '+DATA' size 200m autoextend on next 50m maxsize 400m;

If you are not using ASM, use an actual directory path instead of +DATA, such as:

SQL> create smallfile tablespace tbs_shrink_test
datafile '/u01/app/oracle/oradata/ORCL/PDB/tbs_shrink_test01.dbf' size 200m autoextend on next 50m maxsize 400m;

Create a user and the necessary grants:

SQL> create user shrinku identified by "Welcome_26ai"
default tablespace tbs_shrink_test
temporary tablespace temp
quota unlimited on tbs_shrink_test;

grant create session, create table, create procedure, create sequence to shrinku;
grant execute on dbms_space to shrinku;
grant select_catalog_role to shrinku;

Connect as newly created user to the target database and create a churn table:

sqlplus shrinku/"Welcome_26ai"@localhost:1521/PDB

SQL> create table t1_churn (
 id        number generated always as identity,
 pad       varchar2(4000), 
 upd_flag  number,
 created_on date default sysdate
) tablespace tbs_shrink_test;
Insert enough rows to consume space. Do the bulk insert:
SQL> insert /*+ append */ into t1_churn(pad, upd_flag)
select rpad('X', 3900, 'X'), 0
from   dual
connect by level <= 30000;   

commit;

Create some waste inside the segment by doing the following DML operations:

SQL> update t1_churn
set    upd_flag = 1
where  mod(id, 3) = 0;
delete from t1_churn
where  mod(id, 2) = 0;
commit;

3)	Add a second datafile to the existing tablespace

Connect as SYS user and run:

sqlplus / as sysdba
SQL> alter session set container=pdb;

Add the second datafile to tbs_shrink_test tablespace:

SQL> alter tablespace tbs_shrink_test
add datafile '+DATA' size 300m autoextend on next 50m maxsize 800m;

If you have datafiles on filesystems, then indicate the path:

alter tablespace tbs_shrink_test
add datafile '/u01/app/oracle/oradata/ORCL/PDB/tbs_shrink_test02.dbf' size 300m autoextend on next 50m maxsize 800m;

Create a second table to push extents into

Connect as SHRINKU user:

sqlplus shrinku/"Welcome_26ai"@localhost:1521/PDB

Create T2 table and fill it with data:

SQL> create table t2_big (
  id  number,
  pad varchar2(4000)
) tablespace tbs_shrink_test;
SQL> insert /*+ append */ into t2_big(id, pad)
select level, rpad('Y', 3900, 'Y')
from   dual
connect by level <= 60000; 
commit;

Create a free file scenario

Stay connected as SHRINKU  user and drop T2 table to create “free file” scenario:

SQL> drop table t2_big purge;

Do baseline checks before shrink
As SYS user, check datafile sizes:

SQL> col file_name for a60

SQL> select 
       file_name,
       round(bytes/1024/1024) as mb
from   dba_data_files
where  tablespace_name = 'TBS_SHRINK_TEST';

Check how much segment space is used in the tablespace:

SQL> col segment_name for a30
SQL> select segment_name, round(bytes/1024/1024) as mb
     from   dba_segments
     where  tablespace_name = 'TBS_SHRINK_TEST'
     order  by mb desc;

For each datafile in TBS_SHRINK_TEST, check how big the file is right now, and how much space inside it is currently free, unallocated to any segment:

SQL> select df.file_id,
       round(df.bytes/1024/1024) as df_mb,
       round(nvl(fs.free_bytes,0)/1024/1024) as free_mb
from   dba_data_files df
left join(
    select file_id, sum(bytes) free_bytes
    from   dba_free_space
    where  tablespace_name = 'TBS_SHRINK_TEST'
    group  by file_id
 ) fs on fs.file_id = df.file_id
where  df.tablespace_name = 'TBS_SHRINK_TEST'
order  by df.file_id;

Run SHRINK_TABLESPACE in ANALYZE mode with report
As SYS user, execute dbms_space.shrink_tablespace procedure to analyze and print the returned CLOB:

SQL> set serveroutput on
 declare
  l_result clob;
 begin
  dbms_space.shrink_tablespace(
   ts_name        => 'TBS_SHRINK_TEST',
   shrink_mode    => dbms_space.ts_shrink_mode_analyze,
   target_size    => dbms_space.ts_target_max_shrink,
   shrink_result  => l_result,
   iterations     => 1,
   drop_datafiles => false
);
 dbms_output.put_line(dbms_lob.substr(l_result, 32767, 1));

end;
/

Run ONLINE shrink and capture the report

Connect as SYS user and run:

set serveroutput on
 declare
   l_result clob;
begin
  dbms_space.shrink_tablespace(
    ts_name        => 'TBS_SHRINK_TEST',
    shrink_mode    => dbms_space.ts_shrink_mode_online,
    target_size    => dbms_space.ts_target_max_shrink,
    shrink_result  => l_result,
    iterations     => 1,        -- start with 1 for a gentle test
    drop_datafiles => false     -- keep files for now
  );

 dbms_output.put_line(dbms_lob.substr(l_result, 32767, 1));
end;
/

Verify size after shrink

For each datafile in TBS_SHRINK_TEST, check how big the file is right now, and how much space inside it is currently free, unallocated to any segment:

SQL> select df.file_id,
       round(df.bytes/1024/1024) as df_mb,
       round(nvl(fs.free_bytes,0)/1024/1024) as free_mb
from   dba_data_files df
left join(
    select file_id, sum(bytes) free_bytes
    from   dba_free_space
    where  tablespace_name = 'TBS_SHRINK_TEST'
    group  by file_id
 ) fs on fs.file_id = df.file_id
where  df.tablespace_name = 'TBS_SHRINK_TEST'
order  by df.file_id;

Let’s test DROP_DATAFILES is TRUE option

If the second file becomes empty after moves, Oracle can drop it if we indicate drop_datafiles => true:

Connect as SYS user and run:

SQL> set serveroutput on
SQL> declare
   l_result clob;
begin
  dbms_space.shrink_tablespace(
    ts_name        => 'TBS_SHRINK_TEST',
    shrink_mode    => dbms_space.ts_shrink_mode_online,
    target_size    => dbms_space.ts_target_max_shrink,
    shrink_result  => l_result,
    iterations     => 1,        
    drop_datafiles => true     
  );

 dbms_output.put_line(dbms_lob.substr(l_result, 32767, 1));
end;
/

Verify tablespace after shrinking with drop

Connect as SYS user and run:

SQL> select df.file_id,
       round(df.bytes/1024/1024) as df_mb,
       round(nvl(fs.free_bytes,0)/1024/1024) as free_mb
from   dba_data_files df
left join(
    select file_id, sum(bytes) free_bytes
    from   dba_free_space
    where  tablespace_name = 'TBS_SHRINK_TEST'
    group  by file_id
 ) fs on fs.file_id = df.file_id
where  df.tablespace_name = 'TBS_SHRINK_TEST'
order  by df.file_id;

Readd a second datafile to the existing tablespace

Since the datafile was dropped by the previous test, we need to re-add the datafile to the tablespace.

Connect as SYS user:

sqlplus / as sysdba

SQL> alter session set container=pdb;

Add the second datafile to tbs_shrink_test tablespace:

alter tablespace tbs_shrink_test
add datafile '+DATA' size 300m autoextend on next 50m maxsize 800m;

If you have datafiles on filesystems, then indicate the path:

alter tablespace tbs_shrink_test
add datafile '/u01/app/oracle/oradata/ORCL/PDB/tbs_shrink_test02.dbf' size 300m autoextend on next 50m maxsize 800m;

Create a table that we will pin into the new datafile

Connect as SHRINKU and run:

SQL> create table t_move_demo(
  id  number,
  pad varchar2(4000)
) tablespace tbs_shrink_test;

Now force extent allocations into file #15 using ALLOCATE EXTENT ... DATAFILE:

SQL> alter table t_move_demo allocate extent(
  size 64m
  datafile '/u01/app/oracle/oradata/ORCL/PDB/tbs_shrink_test02.dbf'
);

SQL> alter table t_move_demo allocate extent(
  size 64m
  datafile '/u01/app/oracle/oradata/ORCL/PDB/tbs_shrink_test02.dbf'
);

Put some data in it 

Connect as SHRINKU and run:

SQL> insert /*+ append */ into t_move_demo
select level, rpad('M', 3900, 'M')
from dual
connect by level <= 20000;

commit;

Prove that T_MOVE_DEMO has extents in the new datafile

The output from the following query proves the table T_MOVE_DEMO is physically spread across multiple datafiles #14 and #16, so object movement is now possible, and likely required, if you try to shrink the tablespace and especially if you want to drop an empty file.

SQL> col segment_name for a30
SQL> select segment_name, 
            file_id, 
            round(sum(bytes)/1024/1024) mb
from   dba_extents
where  owner = 'SHRINKU'
and    segment_name = 'T_MOVE_DEMO'
group  by segment_name, file_id
order  by file_id;

Re-check free space by file

Run the following query to determine how much space is allocated and how much is free:

SQL> select df.file_id,
       round(df.bytes/1024/1024) as df_mb,
       round(nvl(fs.free_bytes,0)/1024/1024) as free_mb
from   dba_data_files df
left join(
    select file_id, sum(bytes) free_bytes
    from   dba_free_space
    where  tablespace_name = ‘TBS_SHRINK_TEST’
    group  by file_id
) fs on fs.file_id = df.file_id
where  df.tablespace_name = ‘TBS_SHRINK_TEST’
order  by df.file_id;

Resize the datafile #14

To better achieve our desired test scenario, resize datafile 14 to provide space for extent movement:

SQL> alter database datafile 14 resize 600m;

Run ANALYZE again 

Run the dbms_space.shrink_tablespace procedure with the following parameters. Now movement should be possible.

Connect as SYS user and run:

SQL> set serveroutput on
declare
  l_result clob;
begin
  dbms_space.shrink_tablespace(
    ts_name        => 'TBS_SHRINK_TEST',
    shrink_mode    => dbms_space.ts_shrink_mode_online,
    target_size    => dbms_space.ts_target_max_shrink,
    shrink_result  => l_result,
    iterations     => 3,
    drop_datafiles => true
  );
  dbms_output.put_line(dbms_lob.substr(l_result, 32767, 1));
end;
/

Check the free space

Rerun the previous query to check free space per datafile:

SQL> select df.file_id,
       round(df.bytes/1024/1024) as df_mb,
       round(nvl(fs.free_bytes,0)/1024/1024) as free_mb
from   dba_data_files df
left join(
    select file_id, sum(bytes) free_bytes
    from   dba_free_space
    where  tablespace_na  2  me = 'TBS_SHRINK_TEST'
    group  by file_id
) fs on fs.file_id = df.file_id
where  df.tablespace_name = 'TBS_SHRINK_TEST'
order  by df.file_id;


Check the segment allocation

Confirm where the table’s extents physically live after the shrink:

SQL> col segment_name for a30
SQL> select segment_name, 
            file_id, 
            round(sum(bytes)/1024/1024) mb
from   dba_extents
where  owner = 'SHRINKU'
and    segment_name = 'T_MOVE_DEMO'
group  by segment_name, file_id
order  by file_id;

